             ("Binary search needs order", "Binary search maintains a sorted search interval. Compare the target with the middle value and discard the half that cannot contain it. Repeat until a match is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThe interval halves each step, giving O(log n) comparisons. Efficient implementation assumes fast access to the midpoint, as in an array. Binary search does not work correctly on an arbitrary unsorted array.", "concept"),
             ("Worked example: halve the interval", "Generated practice example: search for 18 in [2, 5, 9, 12, 18, 24, 30].\n\n1. The middle value is 12. Since 18 is larger, keep the right interval.\n2. The middle of [18, 24, 30] is 24. Keep its left interval.\n3. Compare with 18 and return the matching index.\n\nUse consistent inclusive or half-open bounds. Mixing conventions is a common source of off-by-one errors.", "example"),
